# modules.cpp test tool specification

# Status and authority

This document specifies the implemented test command, host and target lane selection, compile-only operation, capability validation, and exit propagation.

docs/modules-configure.mdy remains authoritative for configured lanes and capability resolution. docs/modules-build.mdy owns the shared meaning of per-invocation host and target selection. This document owns test selection, test artifacts, execution, and runner behavior.

# Command line

test [-v or --verbose] [-h or --help] [--host or --target] [--compile-only] test-manifest

The required positional argument is a registered kind:test mm.mdy manifest or its directory. Test has no default manifest because running every test is the wrapper script's responsibility.

-v and --verbose enable configuration and compiler command logging. -h and --help print the command syntax and exit successfully without requiring a test manifest or configuration.

--host and --target are mutually exclusive. Supplying both, repeating either, supplying more than one manifest, or supplying an unknown option is a usage error and exits 64.

--compile-only compiles and links the selected test but does not execute it. It is valid for either lane and is especially useful before a target runner is configured.

Test accepts no compiler, compiler version, target triple, build type, compiler flag, linker flag, runner command, or output-directory override.

# Lane selection

Test uses lanes already defined by configure. Without --host or --target it follows the selection persisted in out/config.mdy.

--host selects the configured host toolchain and buildable-host for this invocation.

--target selects the configured target toolchain and buildable-target for this invocation.

For a target configured with --target-host, target selection admits the union of buildable-host and buildable-target. This permits compile-only production of host-capability test artifacts for a hosted target without changing their manifests.

Selection is temporary. Test does not rewrite configuration or resolved option records.

--target without a configured target lane exits 65. No target compiler falls back to the host compiler.

# Capability enforcement

Test resolves capability from the project root through the same resolver used by configure and build, including assignment, reset, inheritance, and read-only rules.

The requested test must be available in the selected lane. An unavailable requested test exits 77 rather than becoming a silent success because the user named that test explicitly. The distinct status lets test.sh classify a suite it selected itself as skipped without capturing or parsing the tool's output.

Example diagnostic:

test: tests/device/mm.mdy: device is not buildable-target

Every module reached through use must be available in the same lane. Capability incompatibility is rejected before compilation and identifies the consumer, dependency, lane, and restricting origin.

Capability declarations produce no ignored-option warnings. Other manifest options retain their warning-only behavior until compilation consumes them.

# Compile and link

Test appends the requested kind:test target to the selected project modules, orders only its transitive dependency closure, compiles its unit entries, and links one test executable.

The selected configured toolchain supplies the compiler and linker. The test command cannot select a different compiler or build type from build.

--compile-only completes after a successful link and returns 0. It prints no Run phase and invokes neither a host binary nor a target runner.

# Host execution

For a host lane without --compile-only, test runs the linked executable through mm.run and returns the test program's status.

A failure to start the executable exits 127. A nonzero status returned by the test program is propagated.

# Target execution

A target executable must never be run directly as a host program merely because it is a local file.

A target configured with a runner executes its linked test through that runner and propagates the resulting status. Runner program, ordered arguments, executable-image placement, and application-argument forwarding are configuration rather than test options.

A target without a runner rejects execution before compilation and reports:

test: target TARGET-TRIPLE has no test runner; use --compile-only

The target binary is never executed directly. --compile-only remains valid whether or not the target has a runner and never invokes it.

# Output directories

Host test artifacts live under:

out-host/tests/TEST-NAME

Target test artifacts live under the configured target lane, for example:

out-target-aarch64-linux-gnu/tests/TEST-NAME

An unconfigured legacy host test may continue to use out/tests/TEST-NAME.

The test binary, objects, compiler module artifacts, and temporary link output remain within that directory. Test never installs its executable into out/bin.

# Diagnostics and exit status

- 0 means compilation, linking, and any requested execution succeeded.
- 64 means invalid command-line usage.
- 65 means invalid manifest, configuration, capability, lane, or missing target runner.
- 77 means the requested test is not buildable in the selected lane.
- 80 means compilation failed.
- 81 means linking failed.
- 127 means a host test executable could not be started.

Verbose logging prints the selected lane, compiler, build type, flags, output directory, unit count, dependency count, and any executed host command.

# Examples

Run a host test regardless of the configured default lane:

./test --host tests/mm/build

Compile and link a target test without executing it:

./test --target --compile-only tests/device

# Conformance

- Test without a selector follows the persisted default lane.
- --host uses only the configured host toolchain, buildable-host, and host test directory.
- --target uses only the configured target toolchain, buildable-target, and target test directory.
- Explicit selection changes no persisted configuration or option record.
- Both lane selectors together and repeated selectors exit 64.
- --target without a target lane exits 65 before compilation.
- An explicitly requested test unavailable in the lane exits 77 and names that lane.
- An unavailable dependency is rejected before compilation.
- --compile-only compiles and links but invokes no executable or runner.
- Target execution without a runner exits 65 and never executes the target binary directly.
- Host execution continues to propagate the local test status.
- Test exposes no compiler or build-type override.

# Current boundary

Explicit --host, --target, and --compile-only selection and runner-backed target execution are implemented. A target without a configured runner requires --compile-only.
